    "<p><strong>Geometric applications</strong> (functions continuous on $[a, b]$):</p>\n       <ul><li>area of the region between the graphs of $f$ and $g$: $S=\\int_a^b |f(x)-g(x)|\\,dx$,</li>\n       <li>volume of the solid obtained by rotating the region under the graph of $f$ about the $x$-axis: $V=\\pi\\int_a^b f^2(x)\\,dx$,</li>\n       <li>length of the graph of a function with a continuous derivative: $\\ell=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{1+f'^2(x)}\\,dx$,</li>\n       <li>area of a surface of revolution: $P=2\\pi\\int_a^b |f(x)|\\sqrt{1+f'^2(x)}\\,dx$.</li></ul>",
    "<p><strong>Improper integrals.</strong> For an unbounded interval we define $\\int_a^{\\infty} f(x)\\,dx=\\lim_{b\\to\\infty}\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx$; if $f$ is unbounded near the point $a$, then $\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx=\\lim_{c\\to a^+}\\int_c^b f(x)\\,dx$.\n       If the limit is finite, the integral <strong>converges</strong>; otherwise it diverges. Basic comparison integrals:</p>\n       <p style=\"text-align:center\">$\\displaystyle\\int_1^{\\infty}\\frac{dx}{x^p}=\\frac{1}{p-1}$ for $p\\gt 1$ (diverges for $p\\le 1$),&nbsp;&nbsp; $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{1}\\frac{dx}{x^p}=\\frac{1}{1-p}$ for $p\\lt 1$ (diverges for $p\\ge 1$).</p>\n       <p><strong>Comparison test:</strong> if $0\\le f\\le g$ on $[a, \\infty)$ and $\\int_a^\\infty g$ converges, then $\\int_a^\\infty f$ converges too; if $\\int_a^\\infty f$ diverges, then $\\int_a^\\infty g$ diverges too.</p>"
