  "$\\dfrac{{@0}}{n+{@1}}=1+\\dfrac{{@2}}{n+{@3}}$. Pre $x_n={@4}$ platí $|x_n|\\to\\infty$ a exponent ${@5}=x_n\\cdot\\dfrac{{@6}n}{n+{@7}}$, kde $\\dfrac{{@8}n}{n+{@9}}\\to {@10}$. Preto limita je $\\mathrm{e}^{{@11}}$ a $k={@12}$.": "$\\dfrac{{@0}}{n+{@1}}=1+\\dfrac{{@2}}{n+{@3}}$. For $x_n={@4}$ we have $|x_n|\\to\\infty$ and the exponent ${@5}=x_n\\cdot\\dfrac{{@6}n}{n+{@7}}$, where $\\dfrac{{@8}n}{n+{@9}}\\to {@10}$. Therefore the limit is $\\mathrm{e}^{{@11}}$ and $k={@12}$.",
  "$\\displaystyle S=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{{@0}(1+\\cos\\varphi)} r\\,dr\\,d\\varphi=\\frac{{@1}}{2}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(1+2\\cos\\varphi+\\cos^2\\varphi\\right)d\\varphi$. Keďže $\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\cos\\varphi\\,d\\varphi=0$ a $\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\cos^2\\varphi\\,d\\varphi=\\pi$, je $S=\\frac{{@2}}{2}(2\\pi+\\pi)={@3}\\,\\pi$, teda $k={@4}$.": "$\\displaystyle S=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{{@0}(1+\\cos\\varphi)} r\\,dr\\,d\\varphi=\\frac{{@1}}{2}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(1+2\\cos\\varphi+\\cos^2\\varphi\\right)d\\varphi$. Since $\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\cos\\varphi\\,d\\varphi=0$ and $\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\cos^2\\varphi\\,d\\varphi=\\pi$, we get $S=\\frac{{@2}}{2}(2\\pi+\\pi)={@3}\\,\\pi$, so $k={@4}$.",
